# LSAT Practice Test 1. Original items. Key first in every option list (see build.py).

MC = "Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?"
FLAW = "The argument's reasoning is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that the argument:"
NEC = "The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?"
SUF = "The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?"
STR = "Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?"
WEAK = "Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the argument?"
EXPL = "Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?"
MUST = "If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?"
MSP = "The statements above, if true, most strongly support which one of the following?"
PAR = "Which one of the following arguments is most similar in its pattern of reasoning to the argument above?"
PARFLAW = "The flawed pattern of reasoning in the argument above is most similar to that in which one of the following?"
PRIN = "Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle stated above?"

STIMULI = [
    {"id": "LSAT-T1-P1", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "In the eighteenth century, a soloist performing a concerto was expected near the end of a movement to play a cadenza, a passage "
        "in which the orchestra fell silent and the soloist elaborated on the movement's themes. Composers usually marked only where the "
        "cadenza should begin. What the soloist played was left to the performer, who might improvise on the spot or prepare something in "
        "advance. Audiences listened to a cadenza less as a part of the composer's work than as a display of the performer's invention.\n\n"
        "Over the following century this arrangement changed. Composers increasingly wrote out their own cadenzas, and performers came to "
        "regard the written score as a complete statement of the work. Today most soloists play a cadenza written by the composer or by a "
        "famous performer of the past, and a soloist who improvises one is rare enough to be remarked upon. Many music historians describe "
        "this shift as a loss: an art of improvisation that once belonged to every trained performer, they argue, was surrendered to the "
        "authority of the printed page.\n\n"
        "That account is not wrong, but it is incomplete. The written cadenza did not simply replace improvisation; it also changed what "
        "audiences could hear. A performer improvising under the pressure of a concert tended to rely on familiar figures, the runs and "
        "trills that fit comfortably under the hand. A composer writing a cadenza at a desk could instead build it from the movement's "
        "own material, developing a theme in ways that the rest of the movement had only suggested. Some of the most admired cadenzas "
        "are admired for precisely this reason: they read less as interruptions than as a culmination of the movement.\n\n"
        "None of this means that today's soloists should abandon written cadenzas, nor that they must return to improvising. It means "
        "that the history of the cadenza is better told as an exchange than as a loss. Performers gave up one kind of freedom, and "
        "listeners gained a kind of coherence that improvisation rarely achieved. A performer who chooses to improvise today is not "
        "recovering a lost purity; she is choosing one set of rewards over another.")},
    {"id": "LSAT-T1-P2", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Before a jury begins deliberating, the judge reads it a set of instructions that explain the law the jurors must apply. In "
        "many courts these instructions are drawn from model texts written decades ago by committees of judges and lawyers. The texts "
        "are precise, but their precision is the precision of legal drafting: long sentences, technical terms such as \"preponderance of "
        "the evidence,\" and double negatives that a lawyer parses easily and a juror may not parse at all.\n\n"
        "Researchers who have tested such instructions on mock jurors have repeatedly found that many jurors misunderstand them. In some "
        "studies, a large share of participants could not correctly restate the standard of proof they had just been read. The same "
        "studies found that rewritten instructions, using short sentences and everyday words, produced markedly better comprehension "
        "without changing the legal rule being explained.\n\n"
        "Objections to rewriting have generally taken two forms. The first is that plain language sacrifices accuracy: everyday words, "
        "the argument goes, carry connotations that technical terms were chosen to avoid. The second is practical. Model instructions "
        "have been tested in appeals for years, and courts know which phrasings survive review. A court that adopts new wording invites "
        "appeals arguing that the new text misstates the law.\n\n"
        "Neither objection is decisive. The first assumes that technical terms convey their intended meaning to jurors, which is "
        "exactly what the research calls into question; a phrase that is accurate on the page but misunderstood in the jury box is not "
        "accurate in any sense that matters to a verdict. The second objection has more force, but it describes a cost of transition "
        "rather than a permanent defect. Several jurisdictions that adopted plain-language instructions have reported that, after an "
        "initial period, appeals based on the new wording became uncommon.\n\n"
        "What the objections do show is that rewriting should be done with care. Instructions should be revised by drafting committees "
        "that include people trained in testing comprehension, and revised texts should be tried on mock jurors before courts adopt "
        "them. Done that way, plain language is not a concession to jurors' limitations. It is the only means of ensuring that the law "
        "the jury applies is the law the court intended.")},
    {"id": "LSAT-T1-P3", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Beneath many forests, the roots of trees are joined by threads of fungi that wrap around or penetrate the root cells. These "
        "mycorrhizal fungi draw sugars from the trees and in return deliver water and mineral nutrients that their threads gather from "
        "the soil. Because a single fungus can connect to the roots of several trees, researchers have described forests as linked by "
        "underground networks, and some have proposed that trees use these networks to pass resources to one another, with large, "
        "established trees supporting seedlings growing in their shade.\n\n"
        "The idea has spread well beyond the scientific literature. Popular accounts describe parent trees nursing their offspring and "
        "forests behaving like cooperative communities. Several ecologists have recently argued that these accounts have outrun the "
        "evidence. They point out that the experiments most often cited were small, that many were carried out in greenhouses or with "
        "potted seedlings rather than in mature forests, and that carbon labeled in one tree and later detected in another might have "
        "traveled through the soil rather than through fungal threads. In some field studies, moreover, seedlings connected to the "
        "network of a large tree grew no better, and sometimes worse, than unconnected seedlings.\n\n"
        "These critics do not deny that mycorrhizal networks exist, nor that resources can move through them. Their claim is narrower: "
        "that the evidence does not yet show that such transfers are large enough or common enough to shape how forests grow, and that "
        "it says nothing about whether trees direct resources toward particular neighbors. A fungus that moves carbon from one root to "
        "another may simply be moving it where its own growth requires, with the trees involved playing no active role.\n\n"
        "The debate matters beyond botany. Forest managers in some regions have begun to recommend leaving large trees standing during "
        "logging partly on the ground that they nourish the surrounding seedlings. There may be good reasons for that practice, since "
        "large trees provide shade, seed and habitat. But if the practice is to be justified by the network hypothesis in particular, "
        "the hypothesis will need to be tested where it would be applied: in mature forests, over years, with measurements designed to "
        "tell transfer through fungi from movement through soil.")},
    {"id": "LSAT-T1-P4", "title": "Passage A and Passage B", "text": (
        "**Passage A**\n\n"
        "Most scientific research is paid for by taxpayers, yet much of it is published in journals that charge readers for access. A "
        "nurse who wants to read a study of the treatment she gives every day, or a teacher who wants to check a claim about how children "
        "learn to read, may face a fee for each article. This arrangement makes the public pay twice: once to fund the research and again "
        "to read its results. Research funded by public money should be free to read as soon as it is published. Funding agencies can "
        "make this happen simply by requiring it as a condition of their grants.\n\n"
        "**Passage B**\n\n"
        "Few people dispute that publicly funded research ought to be freely available. The difficulty lies in how the costs of "
        "publishing are paid once readers no longer pay them. Editing, peer review and hosting are not free, and many journals that "
        "have dropped reader fees now charge authors a fee for each article they publish. For a well-funded laboratory such fees are a "
        "small line in a grant. For a researcher at a poorly funded university, or in a country where the fee equals months of salary, "
        "they can decide whether a study is published at all. A rule that makes articles free to read without asking who pays to publish "
        "them may simply move the barrier from the reader to the author. Funders that require open access should also pay, or arrange "
        "waivers for, the fees their requirement creates.")},
]

